window.NOTES=[{"id":"prophets_in_1_2_kings","title":"Prophets in 1 & 2 Kings","sum":"Every true and false prophet in Kings, with references, key events and significance.","ico":"🔥","secs":[{"t":"Overview","h":"\n<p>1 and 2 Kings cover roughly 400 years of Israel's history, from Solomon's reign to the Babylonian exile. Prophets are central to the narrative — they anoint and depose kings, pronounce judgment, perform miracles, and call the nation back to covenant faithfulness. Below is every prophet (true and false) named or referenced in these two books, in the order they appear.</p>\n"},{"t":"1 Kings","h":"<h1>1 Kings</h1>\n<h2>1. Nathan</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 1:8–45</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Court prophet, carried over from David's reign (2 Samuel 7, 12)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Works with Bathsheba to secure Solomon's succession as king over Adonijah's attempted coup. Anoints Solomon at Gihon.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Bridges the reigns of David and Solomon; last active appearance of a prophet from the Davidic court's founding generation.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2. Ahijah the Shilonite</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 11:29–39; 14:1–18</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Prophet from Shiloh</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key Events:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Tears his new cloak into 12 pieces, gives Jeroboam 10, symbolizing the 10 tribes that will be torn from Solomon's house.</p></li>\n<li><p>Later, when Jeroboam's son falls ill, his wife visits Ahijah in disguise; the blind, aged prophet recognizes her by God's revelation and pronounces judgment on Jeroboam's dynasty.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Marks both the rise and the coming fall of Jeroboam's house — a prophet of both blessing and judgment.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. Shemaiah</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 12:21–24</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> \"Man of God\" to Rehoboam</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Stops Rehoboam from waging civil war against the newly divided northern tribes, declaring the division \"from the Lord.\"</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Prevents an early war between Israel and Judah.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. The Unnamed Man of God from Judah</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 13:1–32</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Prophet sent to confront Jeroboam</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Denounces Jeroboam's altar at Bethel; predicts Josiah by name (centuries in advance); the altar splits and Jeroboam's hand withers, then is restored. Deceived by the old prophet of Bethel into disobeying God's command not to eat there; killed by a lion on the road as judgment.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> A sober warning about obeying God's specific instructions, even when persuaded otherwise by a fellow \"prophet.\"</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. The Old Prophet of Bethel</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 13:11–32</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> A prophet living in Bethel (his own integrity is questionable)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Lies to the man of God from Judah to lure him back for a meal, then delivers a genuine prophetic word condemning him for disobedience. Later buries him and asks to be buried in the same grave.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> A morally ambiguous figure — genuine prophetic gift, but used deceptively.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Jehu son of Hanani</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 16:1–7; also 2 Chronicles 19:2; 20:34</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Prophet during the reign of Baasha</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Pronounces judgment on King Baasha for following the sins of Jeroboam; his dynasty will be swept away.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Shows the recurring pattern — each northern dynasty falls under prophetic indictment.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>7. Elijah the Tishbite</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 17–19, 21; 2 Kings 1–2</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> The towering prophet of the divided kingdom, from Gilead</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key Events:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Announces a drought to Ahab (17:1)</p></li>\n<li><p>Fed by ravens, then by the widow of Zarephath; raises her son (17:2–24)</p></li>\n<li><p>Confronts the 450 prophets of Baal at Mount Carmel; fire from heaven; ends the drought (18)</p></li>\n<li><p>Flees Jezebel to Horeb; hears God in \"a still small voice\" (19)</p></li>\n<li><p>Anoints Hazael, Jehu, and Elisha (19:15–16)</p></li>\n<li><p>Confronts Ahab over Naboth's vineyard (21)</p></li>\n<li><p>Confronts Ahaziah, calls down fire on soldiers sent to arrest him (2 Kings 1)</p></li>\n<li><p>Taken up to heaven in a whirlwind/chariot of fire; Elisha receives his mantle (2 Kings 2)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> The prototypical prophet of judgment and covenant zeal; later appears with Moses at the Transfiguration (Matthew 17); Malachi 4:5 promises an \"Elijah\" before the Day of the Lord, fulfilled typologically in John the Baptist.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>8. The Prophets Hidden by Obadiah</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 18:4, 13</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> A group of 100 unnamed prophets</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Hidden in caves by Obadiah (Ahab's palace administrator) and fed during Jezebel's purge of the Lord's prophets.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Shows that Elijah was not literally \"the only one left\" (as he feared, 19:10) — a faithful remnant survived.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>9. Micaiah son of Imlah</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 22:1–28</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> A true prophet who consistently opposed Ahab</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Summoned alongside 400 court prophets before Ahab and Jehoshaphat regarding battle at Ramoth Gilead. Initially answers sarcastically, then delivers a genuine vision of Israel \"scattered like sheep without a shepherd\" and of a deceiving spirit sent to lure Ahab to his death. Struck by Zedekiah and imprisoned for his honesty.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> A model of prophetic courage — telling truth to power regardless of popularity or personal cost.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>10. Zedekiah son of Chenaanah (false prophet)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 22:11, 24</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Leader of Ahab's 400 court prophets</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Makes iron horns symbolizing victory; strikes Micaiah for contradicting him.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Represents the many false, court-flattering prophets contrasted with Micaiah's lone true voice.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>11. The 400 Prophets of Ahab (false prophets)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 1 Kings 22:6, 10–12</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Court prophets who unanimously (falsely) predicted victory</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> All declared victory at Ramoth Gilead; only Micaiah dissented.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Illustrates how numbers and consensus don't guarantee truth.</p></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"t":"2 Kings","h":"<h1>2 Kings</h1>\n<h2>12. Elisha</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 2 Kings 2–13 (extensive)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Elijah's successor; received a \"double portion\" of his spirit</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key Events:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Parts the Jordan; heals the water at Jericho (2:14–22)</p></li>\n<li><p>Multiplies the widow's oil (4:1–7)</p></li>\n<li><p>Raises the Shunammite woman's son (4:8–37)</p></li>\n<li><p>Heals Naaman the Syrian commander of leprosy (5)</p></li>\n<li><p>Makes an axe head float (6:1–7)</p></li>\n<li><p>Strikes Aramean troops with blindness, then shows mercy (6:8–23)</p></li>\n<li><p>Predicts the end of Samaria's famine (7)</p></li>\n<li><p>Anoints Hazael over Aram and instructs the anointing of Jehu over Israel (8–9)</p></li>\n<li><p>Advises Joash on defeating Aram, even prophesying from his deathbed (13:14–21)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> More recorded miracles than any other Old Testament prophet; ministry emphasizes mercy and restoration alongside judgment, often to ordinary and even foreign people.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>13. The Company/Sons of the Prophets</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 2 Kings 2:3–7, 15; 4:1, 38; 6:1–7; 9:1</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Prophetic guilds or schools centered at Bethel, Jericho, and Gilgal, under Elijah's and then Elisha's leadership</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Function as a recognized institution throughout Elisha's ministry, receiving instruction and occasionally needing his help (e.g., the floating axe head).</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Shows prophecy as an organized, communal institution in this period, not solely the work of isolated individuals.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>14. Jonah son of Amittai</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 2 Kings 14:25 (his own book covers his Nineveh mission)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Prophet from Gath Hepher</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> Prophesied that Jeroboam II would restore Israel's border \"from Lebo Hamath to the Sea of the Arabah\" — which happened.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> Only a brief historical mention in Kings, but ties directly to the book of Jonah.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>15. Isaiah son of Amoz</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 2 Kings 19:1–20:19</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Prophet to Judah during Hezekiah's reign</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key Events:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Reassures Hezekiah during Sennacherib's siege of Jerusalem; prophesies Assyria's defeat (19)</p></li>\n<li><p>Heals Hezekiah's fatal illness, gives the sign of the sundial's shadow reversing (20:1–11)</p></li>\n<li><p>Rebukes Hezekiah for showing Judah's treasures to Babylonian envoys, predicting the future exile (20:12–19)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> One of the most significant prophets in the Bible; his own book (Isaiah) greatly expands on this period.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>16. Huldah the Prophetess</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>References:</strong> 2 Kings 22:14–20</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Role:</strong> Prophetess consulted during Josiah's reforms</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Key Events:</strong> After the Book of the Law is found in the Temple, King Josiah sends officials to Huldah rather than any male prophet available at the time (including Jeremiah and Zephaniah, contemporaries). She confirms the coming judgment on Judah but promises Josiah will not personally see it, because of his humble response.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Significance:</strong> A rare and notable example of a woman recognized as the authoritative prophetic voice of her generation.</p></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"t":"Summary Table","h":"<h1>Summary Table</h1>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Prophet</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Book/Chapters</strong></th>\n<th><strong>King(s) Served Under</strong></th>\n<th><strong>True/False</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>Nathan</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 1</th>\n<th>David, Solomon</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Ahijah</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 11, 14</th>\n<th>Solomon, Jeroboam I</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>Shemaiah</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 12</th>\n<th>Rehoboam</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Man of God from Judah</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 13</th>\n<th>Jeroboam I</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>Old Prophet of Bethel</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 13</th>\n<th>Jeroboam I</th>\n<th>Ambiguous</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Jehu son of Hanani</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 16</th>\n<th>Baasha</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>Elijah</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 17-19, 21; 2 Kgs 1-2</th>\n<th>Ahab, Ahaziah</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Hidden 100 Prophets</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 18</th>\n<th>(under Ahab/Jezebel)</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>Micaiah</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 22</th>\n<th>Ahab</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Zedekiah son of Chenaanah</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 22</th>\n<th>Ahab</th>\n<th>False</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>400 court prophets</th>\n<th>1 Kgs 22</th>\n<th>Ahab</th>\n<th>False</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Elisha</th>\n<th>2 Kgs 2-13</th>\n<th>Jehoram-Joash</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>Sons of the Prophets</th>\n<th>2 Kgs 2, 4, 6, 9</th>\n<th>(guild, various kings)</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Jonah</th>\n<th>2 Kgs 14:25</th>\n<th>Jeroboam II</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<th>Isaiah</th>\n<th>2 Kgs 19-20</th>\n<th>Hezekiah</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Huldah</th>\n<th>2 Kgs 22</th>\n<th>Josiah</th>\n<th>True</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"t":"Observations for Further Study","h":"<h1>Observations for Further Study</h1>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Pattern of confrontation:</strong> Nearly every northern king who \"did evil in the eyes of the Lord\" is confronted by a named prophet pronouncing judgment on his dynasty — a recurring literary and theological structure.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>True vs. false prophecy:</strong> 1 Kings 13 and 22 both wrestle directly with how to discern a true prophetic word, even when a fellow \"prophet\" contradicts it or a majority of prophets agree on a message.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Elijah and Elisha as a matched pair:</strong> Elijah's ministry emphasizes confrontation and judgment (fire, drought, wilderness); Elisha's emphasizes provision and mercy (multiplied food, healing, restored life) — together they form one of the richest prophetic narratives in Scripture.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Women in prophecy:</strong> Huldah stands out as the sole named prophetess in Kings, consulted at a pivotal national moment.</p></li>\n</ul>\n"}]},{"id":"kings_after_david","title":"Kings After David","sum":"Judah and Israel rulers: periods, parents, prophets, good and evil deeds.","ico":"👑","secs":[{"t":"Southern Kingdom — Judah (Davidic Line)","h":"<h1 id=\"southern-kingdom-judah-davidic-line\"><strong>Southern Kingdom — Judah (Davidic Line)</strong></h1>\n<p><em>21 rulers, from Solomon to Zedekiah, ending with the Babylonian exile in 586 BC. \"Followed\" = the king he succeeded; \"Died After Whom\" = who reigned after his death.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Time Period (approx. BC)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Parents (Father / Mother)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good / Evil</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Prophet(s)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Became King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Died</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good Deeds</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Evil Deeds</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Solomon</td>\n<td>c. 970–931 BC</td>\n<td>David / Bathsheba</td>\n<td>Good (early); turned Evil (later)</td>\n<td>Nathan (anointed); Ahijah the Shilonite (rebuked)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 1:39; 2:12</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:43</td>\n<td>1 Kings 3:9-12; 8:22-30</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:1-8</td>\n<td>Built the Temple; famed for wisdom; wrote Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Song of Songs; wives turned his heart to idols</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Rehoboam</td>\n<td>c. 931–913 BC</td>\n<td>Solomon / Naamah the Ammonite</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Ahijah the Shilonite (before split); Shemaiah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 12:1; 14:21</td>\n<td>1 Kings 14:31</td>\n<td>2 Chr 11:5-12; 12:6-7 (humbled himself)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 14:22-24</td>\n<td>Kingdom split into Israel and Judah in his reign; Egypt's Shishak invaded Jerusalem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Abijah (Abijam)</td>\n<td>c. 913–911 BC</td>\n<td>Rehoboam / Maacah (Micaiah) daughter of Abishalom</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:1-2</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:7-8</td>\n<td>2 Chr 13:4-12 (rebuked Israel's idolatry)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:3</td>\n<td>Fought and defeated Jeroboam I of Israel (2 Chr 13)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Asa</td>\n<td>c. 911–870 BC</td>\n<td>Abijah / (grandmother Maacah, queen mother)</td>\n<td>Good (early); faltered (late)</td>\n<td>Azariah son of Oded; Hanani the seer</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:9-10</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:24; 2 Chr 16:13</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:11-15; 2 Chr 14:2-5</td>\n<td>2 Chr 16:7-12 (trusted Syria, imprisoned Hanani, diseased feet)</td>\n<td>Removed idols; deposed Maacah as queen mother; reigned 41 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Jehoshaphat</td>\n<td>c. 870–848 BC</td>\n<td>Asa / Azubah daughter of Shilhi</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Jehu son of Hanani; Micaiah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:41</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:50</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:43; 2 Chr 17:3-6</td>\n<td>2 Chr 18:1-3; 19:2 (alliance with Ahab)</td>\n<td>Sent teachers throughout Judah; allied unwisely with Ahab and later Ahaziah</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Jehoram</td>\n<td>c. 848–841 BC</td>\n<td>Jehoshaphat / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Elijah (letter of judgment)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:16-17</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:24; 2 Chr 21:19-20</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:18; 2 Chr 21:4 (killed his brothers)</td>\n<td>Married Athaliah, daughter of Ahab; died of an incurable bowel disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ahaziah</td>\n<td>c. 841 BC (1 yr)</td>\n<td>Jehoram / Athaliah</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>None named directly</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:25-26</td>\n<td>2 Kings 9:27-28</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:27</td>\n<td>Allied with Joram of Israel; killed by Jehu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Athaliah (queen)</td>\n<td>c. 841–835 BC</td>\n<td>Ahab / Jezebel</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Jehoiada the priest opposed her</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:1-3</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:16, 20</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:1 (murdered royal family)</td>\n<td>Only reigning queen of Judah; usurped throne; overthrown by Jehoiada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Joash (Jehoash)</td>\n<td>c. 835–796 BC</td>\n<td>Ahaziah / Zibiah of Beersheba</td>\n<td>Good (while Jehoiada lived); Evil (later)</td>\n<td>Zechariah son of Jehoiada</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:21; 12:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 12:20-21</td>\n<td>2 Kings 12:2-15 (repaired the Temple)</td>\n<td>2 Chr 24:17-22 (killed Zechariah)</td>\n<td>Hidden as infant from Athaliah; murdered by his own servants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Amaziah</td>\n<td>c. 796–767 BC</td>\n<td>Joash / Jehoaddan of Jerusalem</td>\n<td>Good (not wholehearted)</td>\n<td>Unnamed man of God; unnamed prophet</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:1-2</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:19-20</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:3-6</td>\n<td>2 Chr 25:14-16, 27 (worshipped gods of Edom)</td>\n<td>Defeated Edom; foolishly provoked Israel and was defeated; assassinated at Lachish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Uzziah (Azariah)</td>\n<td>c. 767–740 BC</td>\n<td>Amaziah / Jecoliah of Jerusalem</td>\n<td>Good (early); Evil (pride, later)</td>\n<td>Zechariah (unnamed details); Isaiah began ministry at his death</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:1-2</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:7</td>\n<td>2 Chr 26:4-5</td>\n<td>2 Chr 26:16-21 (burned incense, became leprous)</td>\n<td>Strong military and building reign; struck with leprosy for entering the Temple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Jotham</td>\n<td>c. 750–732 BC</td>\n<td>Uzziah / Jerusha daughter of Zadok</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Isaiah, Hosea, Micah (contemporaries)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:32-33</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:38</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:34-35</td>\n<td>None recorded (people still corrupt, v.35)</td>\n<td>Built the upper gate of the Temple; grew powerful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ahaz</td>\n<td>c. 732–716 BC</td>\n<td>Jotham / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Isaiah (Isaiah 7)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16:1-2</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16:20</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16:2-4; 2 Chr 28:1-4 (child sacrifice, idolatry)</td>\n<td>Sought Assyria's help against Israel/Syria; shut the Temple doors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Hezekiah</td>\n<td>c. 716–687 BC</td>\n<td>Ahaz / Abi (Abijah) daughter of Zechariah</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Isaiah</td>\n<td>2 Kings 18:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 20:21</td>\n<td>2 Kings 18:3-7</td>\n<td>2 Kings 20:12-19 (pride before Babylonian envoys)</td>\n<td>Religious reformer; survived Sennacherib's siege; life extended 15 years; built Hezekiah's tunnel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Manasseh</td>\n<td>c. 687–642 BC</td>\n<td>Hezekiah / Hephzibah</td>\n<td>Evil (most wicked); repented late</td>\n<td>Unnamed prophets</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:18</td>\n<td>2 Chr 33:12-16 (late repentance)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:2-9, 16</td>\n<td>Longest reign (55 yrs); taken captive to Babylon, repented and was restored</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Amon</td>\n<td>c. 642–640 BC</td>\n<td>Manasseh / Meshullemeth daughter of Haruz</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:19</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:23</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:20-22</td>\n<td>Assassinated by his own servants after a 2-year reign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Josiah</td>\n<td>c. 640–609 BC</td>\n<td>Amon / Jedidah daughter of Adaiah</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Huldah the prophetess; contemporaries Jeremiah, Zephaniah</td>\n<td>2 Kings 22:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:29-30</td>\n<td>2 Kings 22:2; 23:25 (\"no king like him\")</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>Found the Book of the Law; greatest reformer; killed at Megiddo by Pharaoh Necho</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Jehoahaz (Shallum)</td>\n<td>c. 609 BC (3 mo.)</td>\n<td>Josiah / Hamutal daughter of Jeremiah</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Jeremiah (Jer 22:10-12)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:30-31</td>\n<td>Died captive in Egypt (2 Kings 23:34; Jer 22:11-12)</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:32</td>\n<td>Deposed by Pharaoh Necho after 3 months; taken captive to Egypt, died there</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Jehoiakim (Eliakim)</td>\n<td>c. 609–598 BC</td>\n<td>Josiah / Zebudah daughter of Pedaiah</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Jeremiah (opposed him; burned scroll, Jer 36)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:34-36</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:6</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:37; 24:4</td>\n<td>Burned Jeremiah's scroll; rebelled against Babylon, hastening Judah's fall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Jehoiachin (Jeconiah)</td>\n<td>c. 598–597 BC (3 mo.)</td>\n<td>Jehoiakim / Nehushta daughter of Elnathan</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Jeremiah; Ezekiel</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:6, 8</td>\n<td>Taken captive to Babylon (2 Kings 24:12,15); released 2 Kings 25:27-30</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:9</td>\n<td>Surrendered to Nebuchadnezzar; later released from prison by Evil-Merodach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Zedekiah (Mattaniah)</td>\n<td>c. 597–586 BC</td>\n<td>Josiah / Hamutal daughter of Jeremiah</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Jeremiah</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:17-18</td>\n<td>Captured, blinded, exiled (2 Kings 25:5-7)</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:19-20</td>\n<td>Last king of Judah; rebelled against Babylon; Jerusalem and Temple destroyed, 586 BC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"t":"Northern Kingdom — Israel","h":"<h1 id=\"northern-kingdom-israel\"><strong>Northern Kingdom — Israel</strong></h1>\n<p><em>19 kings across several dynasties, from Jeroboam I to Hoshea, ending with the Assyrian conquest of Samaria in 722 BC.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Time Period (approx. BC)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Parents (Father / Mother)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good / Evil</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Prophet(s)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Became King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Died</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good Deeds</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Evil Deeds</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Jeroboam I</td>\n<td>c. 931–910 BC</td>\n<td>Nebat / Zeruah (a widow)</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Ahijah the Shilonite</td>\n<td>1 Kings 12:20</td>\n<td>1 Kings 14:20</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 12:28-33; 13:33-34</td>\n<td>Set up golden calves at Bethel and Dan; \"the sin of Jeroboam\" became a recurring phrase for later kings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Nadab</td>\n<td>c. 910–909 BC</td>\n<td>Jeroboam I / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Fulfillment of Ahijah's word</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:25</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:27-28</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:26</td>\n<td>Assassinated by Baasha, ending Jeroboam's dynasty (1 Kings 15:29)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Baasha</td>\n<td>c. 909–886 BC</td>\n<td>Ahijah (of Issachar) / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Jehu son of Hanani</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:33</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:6</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:34; 16:2-4, 7</td>\n<td>Exterminated the house of Jeroboam</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Elah</td>\n<td>c. 886–885 BC</td>\n<td>Baasha / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Fulfillment of Jehu's word</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:8</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:9-10</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:13</td>\n<td>Assassinated by Zimri, his chariot commander, while drunk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Zimri</td>\n<td>c. 885 BC (7 days)</td>\n<td>Not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>None named directly</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:15</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:18</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:19</td>\n<td>Shortest reign of any king; killed Elah's household, then took his own life by fire</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Omri</td>\n<td>c. 885–874 BC</td>\n<td>Not named</td>\n<td>Evil (worse than predecessors)</td>\n<td>None named directly</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:21-23</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:28</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:25-26</td>\n<td>Built Samaria as capital; founded a dynasty; notable in extra-biblical records (Mesha Stele)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ahab</td>\n<td>c. 874–853 BC</td>\n<td>Omri / not named</td>\n<td>Evil (worst to that time)</td>\n<td>Elijah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:29</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:37-40</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:30-33; 21:25-26</td>\n<td>Married Jezebel; promoted Baal worship; Naboth's vineyard; killed in battle at Ramoth-Gilead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Ahaziah</td>\n<td>c. 853–852 BC (1 yr)</td>\n<td>Ahab / Jezebel</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Elijah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:51</td>\n<td>2 Kings 1:17</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:52-53</td>\n<td>Consulted Baal-zebub after a fall; died per Elijah's word</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Jehoram (Joram)</td>\n<td>c. 852–841 BC</td>\n<td>Ahab / Jezebel</td>\n<td>Evil (less than parents)</td>\n<td>Elisha</td>\n<td>2 Kings 3:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 9:24</td>\n<td>2 Kings 3:2-3 (removed Baal pillar)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 3:2-3 (continued sin of Jeroboam)</td>\n<td>Killed by Jehu at Jezreel, on Naboth's field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Jehu</td>\n<td>c. 841–814 BC</td>\n<td>Jehoshaphat (father) / Nimshi (grandfather often named)</td>\n<td>Mixed — zealous, yet incomplete</td>\n<td>Elijah (commissioned, 1 Kings 19:16); Elisha (anointed him)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 9:6, 13</td>\n<td>2 Kings 10:35</td>\n<td>2 Kings 10:28-30 (destroyed Baal worship)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 10:29, 31 (kept golden calves)</td>\n<td>Massacred Ahab's house, Jezebel, and Baal worshippers; dynasty promised to 4th generation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Jehoahaz</td>\n<td>c. 814–798 BC</td>\n<td>Jehu / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:9</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:2</td>\n<td>Israel oppressed by Hazael of Syria during his reign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Jehoash (Joash)</td>\n<td>c. 798–782 BC</td>\n<td>Jehoahaz / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Elisha (death-bed scene)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:10</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:13</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:14-19, 25 (visited dying Elisha)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:11</td>\n<td>Defeated Amaziah of Judah (2 Kings 14:8-14)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Jeroboam II</td>\n<td>c. 793–753 BC</td>\n<td>Jehoash (Joash) / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Jonah; contemporaries Amos, Hosea</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:23</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:29</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:25-27 (restored Israel's borders)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:24</td>\n<td>Longest-reigning northern king (41 yrs); prosperity alongside social injustice (per Amos)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Zechariah</td>\n<td>c. 753 BC (6 mo.)</td>\n<td>Jeroboam II / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Fulfillment of word to Jehu</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:8</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:10</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:9</td>\n<td>Last of Jehu's dynasty, fulfilling the 4-generation promise (2 Kings 15:12)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Shallum</td>\n<td>c. 753 BC (1 mo.)</td>\n<td>Jabesh / not named</td>\n<td>Evil (usurper)</td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:13</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:14</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:13-14 (implied, conspirator/usurper)</td>\n<td>One of the shortest reigns; killed by Menahem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Menahem</td>\n<td>c. 752–742 BC</td>\n<td>Gadi / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:17</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:22</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:16, 18 (brutal sack of Tiphsah)</td>\n<td>Paid heavy tribute to Assyria's King Pul (Tiglath-Pileser III), 2 Kings 15:19-20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Pekahiah</td>\n<td>c. 742–740 BC</td>\n<td>Menahem / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:23</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:25</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:24</td>\n<td>Killed by his own captain, Pekah, in the palace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Pekah</td>\n<td>c. 740–732 BC</td>\n<td>Remaliah / not named</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Isaiah (Isa 7:1, Syro-Ephraimite war)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:27</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:30</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:28</td>\n<td>Allied with Syria against Judah; lost territory to Tiglath-Pileser III</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hoshea</td>\n<td>c. 732–722 BC</td>\n<td>Elah / not named</td>\n<td>Evil (less than predecessors)</td>\n<td>None named (Hosea the writing prophet is a contemporary, but distinct person)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:30; 17:1</td>\n<td>Imprisoned by Assyria; Samaria fell, Israel exiled (2 Kings 17:4-6)</td>\n<td>None recorded</td>\n<td>2 Kings 17:2</td>\n<td>Last king of Israel; Samaria fell to Assyria (Shalmaneser V / Sargon II) in 722 BC, ending the Northern Kingdom</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"}]},{"id":"kings_of_judah_israel_syria","title":"Kings of Judah, Israel & Syria","sum":"Detailed reference tables with timelines across the three kingdoms.","ico":"🗺️","secs":[{"t":"Overview","h":"<p><em>A detailed reference chart drawn from 1-2 Kings, 1-2 Chronicles, and the Prophets.</em></p>\n"},{"t":"Southern Kingdom - Judah (House of David)","h":"<h1 id=\"southern-kingdom---judah-house-of-david\"><strong>Southern Kingdom - Judah (House of David)</strong></h1>\n<p><em>From Solomon through Zedekiah, ending with the Babylonian exile in 586 BC.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"identity-reign\"><strong>Identity &amp; Reign</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Parents (Father / Mother)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Yrs Reigned</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Time Period (approx.)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good / Evil</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Killed or Natural Death</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Solomon</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: David</p>\n<p>M: Bathsheba</p></td>\n<td>40 years</td>\n<td>c. 970-931 BC</td>\n<td>Good, later Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death - \"slept with his fathers\" (1 Kings 11:43)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Rehoboam</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Solomon</p>\n<p>M: Naamah (Ammonite)</p></td>\n<td>17 years</td>\n<td>c. 931-913 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (1 Kings 14:31)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Abijah (Abijam)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Rehoboam</p>\n<p>M: Maacah, granddaughter of Absalom</p></td>\n<td>3 years</td>\n<td>c. 913-911 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (1 Kings 15:8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Asa</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Abijah</p>\n<p>M: Maacah (grandmother, retained as queen mother)</p></td>\n<td>41 years</td>\n<td>c. 911-870 BC</td>\n<td>Good, later faltered</td>\n<td>Natural death - diseased in his feet in old age (1 Kings 15:23-24)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoshaphat</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Asa</p>\n<p>M: Azubah</p></td>\n<td>25 years</td>\n<td>c. 870-849 BC</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Natural death (1 Kings 22:50)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoram (Joram)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehoshaphat</p>\n<p>M: Not named</p></td>\n<td>8 years</td>\n<td>c. 849-841 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death, but from an incurable bowel disease (2 Chron 21:18-19)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahaziah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehoram</p>\n<p>M: Athaliah</p></td>\n<td>1 year</td>\n<td>c. 842-841 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - executed by Jehu's men while fleeing (2 Kings 9:27)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Athaliah (Queen)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Ahab</p>\n<p>M: Jezebel</p></td>\n<td>6 years</td>\n<td>c. 841-835 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - executed by the sword at Jehoiada's command (2 Kings 11:16, 20)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoash (Joash)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Ahaziah</p>\n<p>M: Zibiah of Beersheba</p></td>\n<td>40 years</td>\n<td>c. 835-796 BC</td>\n<td>Good, later Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated by his own officials in his bed (2 Chron 24:25)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Amaziah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehoash</p>\n<p>M: Jehoaddan of Jerusalem</p></td>\n<td>29 years</td>\n<td>c. 796-767 BC</td>\n<td>Good, later Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated in a conspiracy at Lachish (2 Kings 14:19)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Uzziah (Azariah)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Amaziah</p>\n<p>M: Jecoliah</p></td>\n<td>52 years</td>\n<td>c. 767-740/750 BC</td>\n<td>Good, later prideful</td>\n<td>Natural death - as a leper, living in isolation (2 Kings 15:5, 7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jotham</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Uzziah</p>\n<p>M: Jerusha</p></td>\n<td>16 years</td>\n<td>c. 740-732 BC</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 15:38)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahaz</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jotham</p>\n<p>M: Not named</p></td>\n<td>16 years</td>\n<td>c. 732-716 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 16:20)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hezekiah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Ahaz</p>\n<p>M: Abijah (Abi)</p></td>\n<td>29 years</td>\n<td>c. 716-687 BC</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Natural death, after 15 added years of life (2 Kings 20:1-6, 21)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Manasseh</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Hezekiah</p>\n<p>M: Hephzibah</p></td>\n<td>55 years</td>\n<td>c. 697/687-643 BC</td>\n<td>Evil, later repented</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 21:18)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Amon</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Manasseh</p>\n<p>M: Meshullemeth</p></td>\n<td>2 years</td>\n<td>c. 642-640 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated by his own officials (2 Kings 21:23)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Josiah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Amon</p>\n<p>M: Jedidah</p></td>\n<td>31 years</td>\n<td>c. 640-609 BC</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Killed - died in battle against Pharaoh Necho at Megiddo (2 Kings 23:29-30)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoahaz (Shallum)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Josiah</p>\n<p>M: Hamutal</p></td>\n<td>3 months</td>\n<td>609 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Died in captivity in Egypt (2 Kings 23:33-34)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoiakim (Eliakim)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Josiah</p>\n<p>M: Zebudah</p></td>\n<td>11 years</td>\n<td>609-598 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Died c. 598 BC - \"slept with his fathers\" (2 Kings 24:6), though Jer 22:18-19 and 36:30 suggest an unceremonious death without proper royal mourning or burial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoiachin (Jeconiah)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehoiakim</p>\n<p>M: Nehushta</p></td>\n<td>3 months</td>\n<td>598-597 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Died in Babylon; exact death not recorded, but released from prison and given honor in his final years (2 Kings 25:27-30)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Zedekiah (Mattaniah)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Josiah</p>\n<p>M: Hamutal</p></td>\n<td>11 years</td>\n<td>597-586 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Died in Babylonian captivity after being blinded; sons killed before him first (2 Kings 25:5-7)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"prophets-key-verses\"><strong>Prophets &amp; Key Verses</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Prophet(s) Who Warned Him</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Became King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Died</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Good</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Evil</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Solomon</strong></td>\n<td>Nathan; Ahijah the Shilonite</td>\n<td>1 Kings 1:39; 2:12</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:43</td>\n<td>1 Kings 3:3, 12</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:4-6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Rehoboam</strong></td>\n<td>Shemaiah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 12:1; 14:21</td>\n<td>1 Kings 14:31</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 14:22-24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Abijah (Abijam)</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:1</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:8</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Asa</strong></td>\n<td>Azariah son of Oded; Hanani the seer</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:9-10</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:24</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:11; 2 Chron 14:2</td>\n<td>2 Chron 16:7-10 (rebuked for relying on Syria, imprisoned Hanani)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoshaphat</strong></td>\n<td>Jehu son of Hanani; Micaiah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:41</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:50</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:43</td>\n<td>2 Chron 19:2 (rebuked for allying with wicked Ahab)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoram (Joram)</strong></td>\n<td>Elijah (letter of judgment)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:16</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:23-24</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahaziah</strong></td>\n<td>None named directly</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:25-26</td>\n<td>2 Kings 9:27-28</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:27</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Athaliah (Queen)</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoiada the priest opposed her</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:1-3</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:13-16, 20</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoash (Joash)</strong></td>\n<td>Zechariah son of Jehoiada (killed for rebuking him)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:21-12:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 12:19-21</td>\n<td>2 Kings 12:2</td>\n<td>2 Chron 24:17-22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Amaziah</strong></td>\n<td>An unnamed man of God (2 Chron 25:7-9)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:1-2</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:19-20</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:3</td>\n<td>2 Chron 25:14, 27</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Uzziah (Azariah)</strong></td>\n<td>Zechariah (instructed him); Isaiah began ministry the year Uzziah died</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:1-2</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:7</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:3</td>\n<td>2 Chron 26:16-21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jotham</strong></td>\n<td>Isaiah, Hosea, Micah (active in this era)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:32-33</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:38</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:34</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahaz</strong></td>\n<td>Isaiah (Isaiah ch. 7)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16:1-2</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16:20</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16:2-4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hezekiah</strong></td>\n<td>Isaiah; Micah</td>\n<td>2 Kings 18:1-2</td>\n<td>2 Kings 20:21</td>\n<td>2 Kings 18:3-6</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Manasseh</strong></td>\n<td>Unnamed prophets (2 Kings 21:10-15); tradition holds Isaiah was martyred under him</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:18</td>\n<td>2 Chron 33:12-13, 15-16 (repentance in captivity)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:2-9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Amon</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:19</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:23-24</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:20-22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Josiah</strong></td>\n<td>Huldah the prophetess; Jeremiah; Zephaniah</td>\n<td>2 Kings 22:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:29-30</td>\n<td>2 Kings 22:2; 23:25</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoahaz (Shallum)</strong></td>\n<td>Jeremiah (lament, Jer 22:10-12)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:30-31</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:33-34</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoiakim (Eliakim)</strong></td>\n<td>Jeremiah (burned his scroll, Jer 36); Uriah the prophet (executed)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:34-36</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:5-6</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:37</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoiachin (Jeconiah)</strong></td>\n<td>Jeremiah (Jer 22:24-30)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:8</td>\n<td>2 Kings 25:27-30</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Zedekiah (Mattaniah)</strong></td>\n<td>Jeremiah; Ezekiel</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:17-18</td>\n<td>2 Kings 25:5-7</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:19</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"narrative-events-fulfilled-prophecies-bible-portions\"><strong>Narrative: Events, Fulfilled Prophecies &amp; Bible Portions</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Died After Which King (Other/Same Kingdom)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Bible Portions About the King (All)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Events in Their Time</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Fulfilled Prophecies</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Solomon</strong></td>\n<td>N/A - reigned before the kingdom divided</td>\n<td>1 Kings 1-11; 1 Chron 28-29; 2 Chron 1-9; Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Song of Songs (attributed to him)</td>\n<td>Built the Temple and his palace; visited by the Queen of Sheba; asked God for wisdom at Gibeon; 700 wives/300 concubines turned his heart to idols in old age.</td>\n<td>Ahijah's prophecy that the kingdom would be torn away (1 Kings 11:11-13, 29-39) was fulfilled after his death when ten tribes revolted (1 Kings 12:15-20).</td>\n<td>Wisest king; kingdom reached its greatest extent and wealth; his idolatry directly caused the division of the kingdom.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Rehoboam</strong></td>\n<td>Jeroboam I (still reigning in Israel)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 12:1-24; 14:21-31; 2 Chron 10-12</td>\n<td>Rejected the elders' counsel and spoke harshly to the people, causing the ten northern tribes to revolt under Jeroboam; Egypt's Pharaoh Shishak invaded and plundered the Temple in his 5th year.</td>\n<td>Ahijah's prophecy of the kingdom splitting (1 Kings 11:29-39) fulfilled through him (1 Kings 12:15-19); Shemaiah's warning not to fight Israel was heeded (1 Kings 12:22-24).</td>\n<td>First king of the divided Southern Kingdom; the split was 'from the LORD' as judgment on Solomon (1 Kings 12:15).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Abijah (Abijam)</strong></td>\n<td>Jeroboam I (still reigning in Israel)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:1-8; 2 Chron 13:1-22</td>\n<td>Warred against Jeroboam I; won a decisive victory at Zemaraim after appealing to God before battle (2 Chron 13:4-20); had 14 wives, 22 sons, 16 daughters.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Walked in his father's sins, yet God still gave him victory for David's sake (1 Kings 15:4-5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Asa</strong></td>\n<td>Ahab (reigning in Israel)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:9-24; 2 Chron 14-16</td>\n<td>Removed idols and deposed his grandmother Maacah for an Asherah pole; defeated Zerah the Cushite's huge army (2 Chron 14:9-13); bribed Ben-hadad I of Syria to attack Israel; late in life relied on physicians rather than God for his disease.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>One of Judah's most thorough early reformers, though his final years show a decline in faith.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoshaphat</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoram of Israel (still reigning)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:1-50; 2 Chron 17-20</td>\n<td>Sent teachers throughout Judah to teach the Law; unwisely allied by marriage/war with Ahab's house; God gave a miraculous victory over Moab/Ammon/Edom at the Valley of Beracah (2 Chron 20); ships built for Ophir were wrecked at Ezion-geber.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Instituted judicial reforms across Judah (2 Chron 19:4-11); one of Judah's most faithful kings, marred mainly by his alliance with Ahab.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoram (Joram)</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoram of Israel (his brother-in-law, still reigning)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:16-24; 2 Chron 21</td>\n<td>Murdered his six brothers to secure the throne; married Athaliah, daughter of Ahab, bringing Baal worship into Judah; Edom and Libnah revolted against him during his reign.</td>\n<td>Elijah's written prophecy of a great plague on his people and a fatal bowel disease on himself (2 Chron 21:12-15) was fulfilled exactly (2 Chron 21:18-19).</td>\n<td>Died to no one's sorrow - 'departed without being desired' and was not buried in the tombs of the kings (2 Chron 21:20).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahaziah</strong></td>\n<td>Jehu (just seized the throne of Israel)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:25-29; 9:27-29; 2 Chron 22:1-9</td>\n<td>Counseled by his mother Athaliah to do evil; joined Jehoram of Israel in battle against Syria; visited wounded Jehoram at Jezreel just as Jehu's coup began.</td>\n<td>His death is described as being 'of God' as part of the judgment on the house of Ahab, to which he was related by marriage (2 Chron 22:7-9).</td>\n<td>Youngest king of Judah at accession (22 in 2 Kings 8:26, though 2 Chron 22:2 has a textual variant of 42); shortest reign of a legitimate king before Athaliah's usurpation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Athaliah (Queen)</strong></td>\n<td>Jehu (reigning in Israel)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:1-20; 2 Chron 22:10-23:21</td>\n<td>Murdered the royal family (her own grandchildren) to seize the throne; the infant Joash was hidden for six years by Jehosheba; Jehoiada organized guards and had her killed in a coup.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Only reigning queen of Judah; the only interruption to the Davidic line's unbroken succession.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoash (Joash)</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoash of Israel (contemporary)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 11:21-12:21; 2 Chron 24</td>\n<td>Crowned at age 7 after being hidden in the Temple; repaired the Temple while priest Jehoiada lived; after Jehoiada's death, fell into idolatry, stoned Jehoiada's son Zechariah in the Temple courtyard; Hazael of Syria threatened Jerusalem and was paid off with Temple treasures.</td>\n<td>Zechariah's dying words, 'The LORD look upon it, and require it' (2 Chron 24:22), were fulfilled when Joash's own servants assassinated him (2 Chron 24:25).</td>\n<td>A tragic reversal - the boy Jehoiada saved later killed Jehoiada's own son.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Amaziah</strong></td>\n<td>Jeroboam II (reigning in Israel)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:1-20; 2 Chron 25</td>\n<td>Executed his father's assassins but spared their children per the Law (2 Kings 14:5-6); defeated Edom in the Valley of Salt, then foolishly worshiped Edom's captured gods; provoked war with Jehoash of Israel, was defeated, captured, and the Temple plundered.</td>\n<td>The man of God warned that bringing Edom's gods home would bring destruction (2 Chron 25:15-16); this was fulfilled in his defeat by Israel and eventual assassination.</td>\n<td>Fled to Lachish after losing the people's confidence, but conspirators pursued and killed him there.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Uzziah (Azariah)</strong></td>\n<td>Menahem / Pekahiah era (reigning in Israel)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:1-7; 2 Chron 26</td>\n<td>Powerful, prosperous 52-year reign; built towers, dug wells, strengthened the army; grew proud, entered the Temple to burn incense himself and was struck with leprosy for life; his son Jotham governed as co-regent afterward.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Isaiah's famous temple vision (Isaiah 6:1) is dated to 'the year that king Uzziah died.'</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jotham</strong></td>\n<td>Pekah (reigning in Israel)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:32-38; 2 Chron 27</td>\n<td>Built the Upper Gate of the Temple and fortified cities; defeated the Ammonites and made them pay tribute; the people, however, continued corrupt worship at the high places.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Considered good, yet Scripture notes Syria and Israel's aggression against Judah began to be sent by the LORD already in his days (2 Kings 15:37).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahaz</strong></td>\n<td>Hoshea (last king of Israel; Samaria fell 722 BC during this era)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16; 2 Chron 28; Isaiah 7-8</td>\n<td>Sacrificed his own son in the fire; closed the Temple and built a pagan altar copied from Damascus; besieged by Syria/Israel (Syro-Ephraimite War) but refused Isaiah's call to trust God, appealed to Assyria instead, becoming its vassal.</td>\n<td>Isaiah's sign of Immanuel, that the two kings (Rezin/Pekah) threatening Ahaz would be brought to nothing before a young child grew up (Isaiah 7:14-16), was fulfilled - Damascus fell in 732 BC, Samaria in 722 BC.</td>\n<td>Judah's darkest king to this point; his Assyrian alliance set Judah on a path of vassalage that lasted generations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hezekiah</strong></td>\n<td>N/A - Northern Kingdom had already fallen (722 BC)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 18-20; 2 Chron 29-32; Isaiah 36-39</td>\n<td>Greatest reformer since David; removed the high places and destroyed the bronze serpent (Nehushtan); survived Sennacherib's siege of Jerusalem after prayer, with the angel of the LORD striking the Assyrian camp; miraculously healed of a fatal illness, sign of the sundial going backward; Samaria fell to Assyria early in his reign (722 BC).</td>\n<td>Isaiah's promise of deliverance from Assyria (2 Kings 19:32-34) was fulfilled overnight (2 Kings 19:35-36); Isaiah's promise of 15 added years (2 Kings 20:6) was fulfilled - he reigned that long afterward.</td>\n<td>Foolishly showed all his treasures to Babylonian envoys, prompting Isaiah's prophecy of future Babylonian captivity (2 Kings 20:12-18).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Manasseh</strong></td>\n<td>N/A - Northern Kingdom already gone</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:1-18; 2 Chron 33:1-20</td>\n<td>Longest-reigning king of Judah; practiced sorcery and child sacrifice, filled Jerusalem with innocent blood, set a carved idol in the Temple itself; taken captive to Babylon in chains, repented, and was restored - removing idols in his final years.</td>\n<td>The prophesied disaster for Judah's idolatry (2 Kings 21:12-15) was ultimately fulfilled at the fall of Jerusalem, though delayed beyond his own lifetime because of his repentance.</td>\n<td>Judah's most wicked king by Scripture's own description (2 Kings 21:11), yet also a rare example of genuine royal repentance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Amon</strong></td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>2 Kings 21:19-26; 2 Chron 33:21-25</td>\n<td>Continued his father's early idolatry without ever repenting; assassinated in his own house by his servants; the people of the land killed the conspirators and made his son Josiah king.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Unlike his father Manasseh, Amon never humbled himself (2 Chron 33:23).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Josiah</strong></td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>2 Kings 22:1-23:30; 2 Chron 34-35</td>\n<td>Became king at age 8; began seeking God at 16, purging idolatry at 20; the Book of the Law was rediscovered in the Temple in his 18th year; led Judah's greatest religious reform and celebrated a landmark Passover; killed intervening against Pharaoh Necho's army at Megiddo.</td>\n<td>An unnamed man of God had prophesied by name over 300 years earlier that a king named Josiah would defile the altar at Bethel (1 Kings 13:2) - fulfilled precisely in 2 Kings 23:15-18. Huldah's prophecy that Josiah would die in peace before seeing the coming disaster (2 Kings 22:18-20) was fulfilled - he died before Jerusalem's destruction.</td>\n<td>Regarded as the last great righteous king of Judah; his death began the kingdom's rapid final decline.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoahaz (Shallum)</strong></td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:30-34; 2 Chron 36:1-4; Jeremiah 22:10-12</td>\n<td>Made king by the people after Josiah's death; deposed after only three months by Pharaoh Necho, who put his brother Jehoiakim on the throne instead and took him captive to Egypt.</td>\n<td>Jeremiah's lament that he would never return to see his native land again (Jer 22:10-12) was fulfilled - he died in Egypt.</td>\n<td>Also called Shallum in Jeremiah 22:11 and 1 Chron 3:15.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoiakim (Eliakim)</strong></td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>2 Kings 23:34-24:6; 2 Chron 36:4-8; Jeremiah 22:13-23; 26; 36</td>\n<td>Installed and renamed by Pharaoh Necho; taxed the people heavily to pay Egypt's tribute; cut up and burned Jeremiah's scroll of prophecy piece by piece; had the prophet Uriah extradited from Egypt and executed; rebelled against Nebuchadnezzar, provoking Babylonian invasion.</td>\n<td>Jeremiah's prophecy that he would be 'buried with the burial of an ass' with no one lamenting him (Jer 22:18-19) is understood to have been fulfilled in the chaos of Babylon's invasion at the end of his reign.</td>\n<td>Judah became a Babylonian vassal state during his reign, beginning the final countdown to exile.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoiachin (Jeconiah)</strong></td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:6-16; 25:27-30; 2 Chron 36:8-10; Jeremiah 22:24-30</td>\n<td>Surrendered to Nebuchadnezzar after only 3 months on the throne; exiled to Babylon along with his mother, officials, and 10,000 craftsmen and nobles; the Temple treasures were stripped; 37 years later, released from prison by Evil-Merodach and given a permanent seat at the king's table.</td>\n<td>Jeremiah's prophecy that he would be childless in terms of ruling descendants on David's throne ('write ye this man childless' - Jer 22:30) was fulfilled - none of his descendants ever reigned again in Judah.</td>\n<td>His release in Babylon (2 Kings 25:27-30) is the very last event recorded in 2 Kings, offering a small note of hope at the book's close.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Zedekiah (Mattaniah)</strong></td>\n<td>N/A - kingdom ended</td>\n<td>2 Kings 24:17-25:7; 2 Chron 36:11-21; Jeremiah 21; 24; 27-29; 32; 34; 37-39; 52</td>\n<td>Last king of Judah, installed by Nebuchadnezzar as a vassal; repeatedly ignored Jeremiah's warnings; rebelled against Babylon; Jerusalem was besieged and fell in 586 BC, the Temple burned - ending the kingdom of Judah.</td>\n<td>Ezekiel prophesied Zedekiah would be brought to Babylon yet not see it with his eyes (Ezek 12:13) - fulfilled exactly: he was blinded right after seeing his sons killed, so Babylon was the last thing his sons' death showed him, but he never visually saw Babylon itself.</td>\n<td>His reign ends both the Davidic monarchy in Jerusalem and the book of 2 Kings' main narrative of the kingdom.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"t":"Northern Kingdom - Israel (Ten Tribes)","h":"<h1 id=\"northern-kingdom---israel-ten-tribes\"><strong>Northern Kingdom - Israel (Ten Tribes)</strong></h1>\n<p><em>From Jeroboam I through Hoshea, ending with the Assyrian conquest of Samaria in 722 BC.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"identity-reign-1\"><strong>Identity &amp; Reign</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Parents (Father / Mother)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Yrs Reigned</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Time Period (approx.)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good / Evil</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Killed or Natural Death</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jeroboam I</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Nebat</p>\n<p>M: Zeruah (not otherwise detailed)</p></td>\n<td>22 years</td>\n<td>c. 931-910 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (1 Kings 14:20)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Nadab</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jeroboam I</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>2 years</td>\n<td>c. 910-909 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated by Baasha at Gibbethon (1 Kings 15:27-28)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Baasha</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Ahijah (of the tribe of Issachar)</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>24 years</td>\n<td>c. 909-886 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (1 Kings 16:6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Elah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Baasha</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>2 years</td>\n<td>c. 886-885 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated while drunk by Zimri (1 Kings 16:9-10)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Zimri</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Not recorded</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>7 days</td>\n<td>885 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Suicide - burned the palace down on himself when besieged by Omri (1 Kings 16:18)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Omri</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Not recorded</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>12 years</td>\n<td>c. 885-874 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (1 Kings 16:28)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahab</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Omri</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>22 years</td>\n<td>c. 874-853 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed in battle by a random arrow at Ramoth-Gilead (1 Kings 22:34-37)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Ahaziah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Ahab</p>\n<p>M: Jezebel</p></td>\n<td>2 years</td>\n<td>c. 853-852 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death, from injuries/illness after a fall (2 Kings 1:17)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoram (Joram)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Ahab</p>\n<p>M: Jezebel</p></td>\n<td>12 years</td>\n<td>c. 852-841 BC</td>\n<td>Evil, though less than his parents</td>\n<td>Killed - shot through the heart by Jehu's arrow (2 Kings 9:24)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehu</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehoshaphat (grandson of Nimshi - not the king of Judah)</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>28 years</td>\n<td>c. 841-814 BC</td>\n<td>Evil (mixed)</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 10:35)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoahaz</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehu</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>17 years</td>\n<td>c. 814-798 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 13:9)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoash (Joash)</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehoahaz</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>16 years</td>\n<td>c. 798-782 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 13:13)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jeroboam II</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jehoash</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>41 years</td>\n<td>c. 793/786-753 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 14:29)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Zechariah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jeroboam II</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>6 months</td>\n<td>c. 753 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated publicly by Shallum (2 Kings 15:10)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Shallum</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Jabesh</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>1 month</td>\n<td>752 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated by Menahem (2 Kings 15:14)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Menahem</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Gadi</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>10 years</td>\n<td>c. 752-742 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Natural death (2 Kings 15:22)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Pekahiah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Menahem</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>2 years</td>\n<td>c. 742-740 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated in the royal citadel by Pekah (2 Kings 15:25)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Pekah</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Remaliah</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>20 years (includes rival/overlapping reign per some chronologies)</td>\n<td>c. 740-732 BC</td>\n<td>Evil</td>\n<td>Killed - assassinated by Hoshea (2 Kings 15:30)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Hoshea</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Elah</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>9 years</td>\n<td>c. 732-722 BC</td>\n<td>Evil (less severe than predecessors)</td>\n<td>Imprisoned by Assyria; ultimate fate/death not recorded (2 Kings 17:4)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"prophets-key-verses-1\"><strong>Prophets &amp; Key Verses</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Prophet(s) Who Warned Him</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Became King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Died</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Good</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Evil</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jeroboam I</strong></td>\n<td>Ahijah the Shilonite</td>\n<td>1 Kings 12:20</td>\n<td>1 Kings 14:20</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 12:28-30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Nadab</strong></td>\n<td>Fulfillment of Ahijah's earlier prophecy</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:25</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:27-28</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Baasha</strong></td>\n<td>Jehu son of Hanani</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:33</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:6</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:34; 16:1-4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Elah</strong></td>\n<td>(Judgment of Jehu son of Hanani against Baasha's house)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:8</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:9-10</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:13</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Zimri</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:10</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:18</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:19</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Omri</strong></td>\n<td>None named directly</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:16, 21-23</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:28</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:25-26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahab</strong></td>\n<td>Elijah; Micaiah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:29</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:34-37</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:30-33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Ahaziah</strong></td>\n<td>Elijah (pronounced his death)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:51</td>\n<td>2 Kings 1:17</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:52-53</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoram (Joram)</strong></td>\n<td>Elisha</td>\n<td>2 Kings 3:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 9:24</td>\n<td>2 Kings 3:2 (removed his father's Baal pillar)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 3:2-3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehu</strong></td>\n<td>Elisha (sent a young prophet to anoint him)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 9:1-13</td>\n<td>2 Kings 10:35</td>\n<td>2 Kings 10:30 (destroyed Baal worship in Israel)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 10:29, 31</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoahaz</strong></td>\n<td>Unnamed (2 Kings 13:4-5, God had compassion)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:9</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoash (Joash)</strong></td>\n<td>Elisha (wept at his deathbed - 2 Kings 13:14-19)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:10</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:13</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jeroboam II</strong></td>\n<td>Jonah (predicted his territorial success); Amos; Hosea</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:23</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:29</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Zechariah</strong></td>\n<td>(Fulfillment of the word given to Jehu, 2 Kings 15:12)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:8</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:10</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Shallum</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:13</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:14</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:13-15 (implied)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Menahem</strong></td>\n<td>Hosea and Amos were active in this era</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:17</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:22</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Pekahiah</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:23</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:25</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Pekah</strong></td>\n<td>Isaiah; Oded (2 Chron 28:9-11)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:27</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:30</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:28</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Hoshea</strong></td>\n<td>Hosea (contemporary prophet)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:30; 17:1</td>\n<td>2 Kings 17:4-6</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 17:2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"narrative-events-fulfilled-prophecies-bible-portions-1\"><strong>Narrative: Events, Fulfilled Prophecies &amp; Bible Portions</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Died After Which King (Other/Same Kingdom)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Bible Portions About the King (All)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Events in Their Time</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Fulfilled Prophecies</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jeroboam I</strong></td>\n<td>Asa (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:26-40; 12:1-33; 13; 14:1-20</td>\n<td>First king of the Northern Kingdom, chosen after leading the revolt against Rehoboam; set up golden calves at Bethel and Dan so people would not worship in Jerusalem - the 'sin of Jeroboam' became the standard for evil kings that followed; his hand withered and was restored when he confronted a man of God at Bethel (1 Kings 13); his son Abijah died as prophesied.</td>\n<td>Ahijah's prophecy that Jeroboam would rule ten tribes (1 Kings 11:29-37) was fulfilled; the man of God's prophecy that Josiah would one day defile Jeroboam's altar (1 Kings 13:2) was fulfilled 300+ years later (2 Kings 23:15-18); Ahijah's later judgment that his whole house would be cut off (1 Kings 14:10-11) was fulfilled by Baasha (1 Kings 15:29).</td>\n<td>Every subsequent northern king is measured against 'the sin of Jeroboam' - none ever removed the golden calves.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Nadab</strong></td>\n<td>Asa (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:25-31</td>\n<td>Assassinated by Baasha while besieging the Philistine city of Gibbethon; Baasha then wiped out the entire house of Jeroboam, leaving no survivor.</td>\n<td>Ahijah's prophecy of complete destruction for Jeroboam's house (1 Kings 14:10-11) was fulfilled here (1 Kings 15:29).</td>\n<td>Reigned only 2 years before the first of many violent coups in Israel's history.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Baasha</strong></td>\n<td>Asa (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:16-16:7</td>\n<td>A military captain who killed Nadab and exterminated all of Jeroboam's line; warred continually with Asa of Judah; fortified Ramah against Judah until Asa bribed Syria to attack him, forcing him to withdraw; condemned by the prophet Jehu for walking in Jeroboam's sin.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Ironically committed the same sin he was raised up to judge, and was condemned with nearly identical language to Jeroboam.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Elah</strong></td>\n<td>Asa (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:6-14</td>\n<td>Assassinated while drunk at the house of his steward Arza in Tirzah by Zimri, commander of half his chariots.</td>\n<td>Jehu's prophecy against Baasha's house (1 Kings 16:1-4) was fulfilled here (1 Kings 16:12).</td>\n<td>Second dynasty in Israel to be completely wiped out, this time by Zimri.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Zimri</strong></td>\n<td>Asa (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:9-20</td>\n<td>Shortest reign of any Israelite king - just seven days; killed the entire house of Baasha; when the army made Omri king instead and besieged Tirzah, Zimri set the palace on fire and died in the flames.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>His brief coup plunged Israel into a civil war between his supporters, Tibni, and Omri.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Omri</strong></td>\n<td>Asa (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:15-28</td>\n<td>Won a civil war against rival claimant Tibni; founded Samaria as the new capital, buying the hill from Shemer; described by Scripture as worse than all before him, yet founded the most powerful and stable dynasty Israel had known.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Founder of the Omride dynasty; his historicity and power are confirmed extra-biblically by the Mesha Stele and Assyrian records, which referred to Israel as 'the House of Omri' for generations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ahab</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoshaphat (allied with him in that same battle, still reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 16:29-22:40</td>\n<td>Married Jezebel and promoted large-scale Baal worship; confronted by Elijah in a 3-year drought and at the Mount Carmel contest; murdered Naboth for his vineyard; fought two wars against Syria's Ben-hadad; died in disguise, struck by a stray arrow exactly as Elijah foretold.</td>\n<td>Elijah's prophecy that dogs would lick Ahab's blood where they licked Naboth's (1 Kings 21:19) was partially fulfilled at his own death (1 Kings 22:38) and fully on his son Jehoram (2 Kings 9:25-26); the prophecy of disaster on his house was fulfilled by Jehu (2 Kings 9-10).</td>\n<td>Considered the most wicked of Israel's kings up to that point (1 Kings 16:30, 33).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Ahaziah</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoshaphat (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 22:51-53; 2 Kings 1:1-18</td>\n<td>Fell through a lattice in his upper room; sent messengers to consult Baal-zebub of Ekron about his recovery instead of the LORD, and was intercepted and rebuked by Elijah; called down fire on two companies of soldiers sent to arrest Elijah; died childless.</td>\n<td>Elijah's word that he would not recover from his bed because he sought Baal-zebub instead of God (2 Kings 1:4, 16) was fulfilled exactly (2 Kings 1:17).</td>\n<td>Reigned only 2 years and left no heir, so his brother Jehoram succeeded him.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoram (Joram)</strong></td>\n<td>Ahaziah of Judah (killed in the same coup)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 3; 6-7; 8:28-29; 9:14-26</td>\n<td>Campaigned against rebelling Moab with Jehoshaphat's help and Elisha's guidance; endured a devastating Syrian siege of Samaria that ended in miraculous deliverance; wounded fighting Syria at Ramoth-Gilead; killed by Jehu at Jezreel and his body thrown onto Naboth's field, fulfilling Elijah's prophecy against Ahab's house.</td>\n<td>Elijah's judgment against Ahab's house (1 Kings 21:21-22, 29) was fulfilled in Jehoram's assassination (2 Kings 9:25-26).</td>\n<td>Last king of the Omride dynasty in Israel; his death and Ahaziah's death happened in the same coup by Jehu.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehu</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoash of Judah (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 9-10</td>\n<td>Anointed in a private ceremony by a prophet sent from Elisha; zealously killed Jehoram, Ahaziah, Jezebel, all 70 of Ahab's sons, and every Baal worshiper he could gather in a deceptive assembly; did not tear down the golden calves of Jeroboam; Hazael of Syria began stripping Israel's eastern territories during his reign.</td>\n<td>Elijah's prophecies against Ahab's house (1 Kings 21:21-24) and against Jezebel specifically (1 Kings 21:23) were fulfilled through Jehu (2 Kings 9:33-37; 10:17).</td>\n<td>Promised a dynasty to the fourth generation for his zeal against Ahab's house (2 Kings 10:30) - this became the longest-lasting dynasty in Israel's history.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jehoahaz</strong></td>\n<td>Jehoash of Judah (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:1-9</td>\n<td>Israel was severely oppressed by King Hazael of Syria throughout his reign as divine judgment, reducing his army to almost nothing; he sought the LORD's favor and was granted a temporary deliverer.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Despite Israel's persistent idolatry, God showed mercy because of His covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob (2 Kings 13:23).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Jehoash (Joash)</strong></td>\n<td>Amaziah (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:10-13, 14-19, 25; 14:8-16</td>\n<td>Wept over the dying Elisha, calling him 'the chariots and horsemen of Israel'; shot an arrow and struck the ground per Elisha's prophetic sign, winning three victories over Syria; defeated and humiliated Amaziah of Judah in battle, breaking down part of Jerusalem's wall.</td>\n<td>Elisha's dying prophecy that he would strike Syria three times (2 Kings 13:18-19) was fulfilled exactly (2 Kings 13:25).</td>\n<td>His limited striking of the ground (3 times instead of 5-6) meant only partial victory over Syria, per Elisha's rebuke.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Jeroboam II</strong></td>\n<td>Uzziah (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 14:23-29; Amos 1:1; 7:9-11; Hosea 1:1</td>\n<td>Longest and most prosperous reign of any northern king; restored Israel's border 'from the entering of Hamath unto the sea of the plain,' near Solomon's extent; beneath the prosperity, Amos and Hosea condemned rampant social injustice, corrupt worship, and exploitation of the poor.</td>\n<td>Jonah's prophecy that he would restore Israel's border to Hamath (2 Kings 14:25) was fulfilled (2 Kings 14:28).</td>\n<td>Israel's most prosperous era, yet also the era most sharply condemned by the writing prophets for injustice.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Zechariah</strong></td>\n<td>Uzziah (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:8-12</td>\n<td>Last king of Jehu's dynasty (fourth generation); assassinated publicly by Shallum in front of the people after only six months.</td>\n<td>God's promise to Jehu of a dynasty to the fourth generation (2 Kings 10:30) was fulfilled exactly and then ended here (2 Kings 15:12).</td>\n<td>His death ended the longest dynasty Israel ever had and plunged the kingdom into a period of rapid, violent instability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Shallum</strong></td>\n<td>Uzziah (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:13-15</td>\n<td>Reigned only one month before being assassinated by Menahem in Samaria.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>One of the shortest reigns in either kingdom, illustrating Israel's descent into political chaos in its final decades.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Menahem</strong></td>\n<td>Uzziah / Jotham (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:16-22</td>\n<td>Brutally attacked Tiphsah, including ripping open pregnant women, for not surrendering; paid heavy tribute to Assyria's King Pul (Tiglath-Pileser III) to secure his throne, beginning Israel's vassalage to Assyria.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>His tribute payment marks the beginning of the end - Assyrian dominance over Israel from this point forward.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Pekahiah</strong></td>\n<td>Jotham (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:23-26</td>\n<td>Assassinated in the royal citadel by his own army officer, Pekah, along with fifty Gileadites.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Continued the chain of violent successions marking Israel's final decades.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Pekah</strong></td>\n<td>Jotham / Ahaz (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:27-31; 37; 16:5; 2 Chron 28:5-15; Isaiah 7</td>\n<td>Allied with Syria's Rezin against Judah in the Syro-Ephraimite War, besieging Jerusalem; the prophet Oded convinced Israel's army to release Judean captives taken in that campaign (2 Chron 28:9-15); Tiglath-Pileser III of Assyria annexed large parts of his territory (Gilead, Galilee) and deported the people.</td>\n<td>Isaiah's prophecy that Israel would be shattered within 65 years (Isaiah 7:8) and that the plot against Judah would not stand (Isaiah 7:7) began to be fulfilled with Assyria's annexation of his territory.</td>\n<td>His territorial losses to Assyria were the first major step toward the Northern Kingdom's final collapse a decade later.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Hoshea</strong></td>\n<td>Ahaz (reigning in Judah)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:30; 17:1-23</td>\n<td>Last king of Israel; became an Assyrian vassal, then rebelled by seeking Egypt's help; imprisoned by Shalmaneser V; Samaria fell after a three-year siege in 722 BC, and Israel was exiled across the Assyrian empire, ending the Northern Kingdom.</td>\n<td>The repeated warnings of prophets and seers over generations that Israel's persistent idolatry would lead to exile (2 Kings 17:13-15, 23) were fully fulfilled with the fall of Samaria.</td>\n<td>2 Kings 17 gives an extended theological explanation for why the Northern Kingdom fell - persistent idolatry despite generations of prophetic warning.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"t":"Syria (Aram-Damascus)","h":"<h1 id=\"syria-aram-damascus\"><strong>Syria (Aram-Damascus)</strong></h1>\n<p><em>Never part of Israel or Judah - an independent, foreign kingdom to the northeast that repeatedly warred with both, until Assyria destroyed it in 732 BC. Many details (parents, exact reign length) are simply not recorded for foreign kings; Scripture does not evaluate them by the 'good/evil' covenant standard applied to Davidic and Israelite kings.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"identity-reign-2\"><strong>Identity &amp; Reign</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Parents (Father / Mother)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Yrs Reigned</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Time Period (approx.)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good / Evil</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Killed or Natural Death</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Rezon</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Eliadah</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified (contemporary of Solomon's reign)</td>\n<td>c. 10th century BC</td>\n<td>N/A - foreign king, not evaluated by covenant standard</td>\n<td>Not recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hezion</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Not recorded</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified</td>\n<td>Uncertain, prior to Ben-hadad I</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Not recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Tabrimon</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Hezion</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified</td>\n<td>Uncertain, prior to Ben-hadad I</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Not recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad I</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Tabrimon (grandson of Hezion)</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified</td>\n<td>c. early-to-mid 9th century BC (contemporary of Asa)</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Not recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad (II)*</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: *Possibly the same king as Ben-hadad I; treated separately here per common popular chronologies</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified (spans Ahab through Jehoram of Israel)</td>\n<td>c. mid 9th century BC</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Killed - murdered (smothered with a wet cloth) by his own officer Hazael (2 Kings 8:15)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hazael</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Not recorded (a court official/servant of Ben-hadad)</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified (multiple decades, spanning Jehu through Jehoahaz of Israel)</td>\n<td>c. 841-c. 800 BC</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Natural death, succeeded by his son (2 Kings 13:24)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad III</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Hazael</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified</td>\n<td>c. early 8th century BC (contemporary of Jehoash of Israel)</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Not recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Rezin</strong></td>\n<td><p>F: Not recorded</p>\n<p>M: Not recorded</p></td>\n<td>Not specified</td>\n<td>c. mid-8th century BC-732 BC</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Killed by Assyrian forces under Tiglath-Pileser III (2 Kings 16:9)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"prophets-key-verses-2\"><strong>Prophets &amp; Key Verses</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Prophet(s) Who Warned Him</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Became King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Died</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Good</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verse - Evil</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Rezon</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:23-24</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:25 (\"an adversary to Israel\")</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hezion</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Tabrimon</strong></td>\n<td>None named</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad I</strong></td>\n<td>Hanani rebuked Asa (not Ben-hadad) for this alliance</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad (II)*</strong></td>\n<td>An unnamed prophet rebuked Ahab for sparing him; Elisha wept prophesying Hazael's cruelty to come</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:7-15</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hazael</strong></td>\n<td>Elisha (prophesied his kingship and the great cruelty he would inflict on Israel)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:15</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:24</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 8:12 (prophesied cruelty); 2 Kings 10:32-33; 13:3, 22 (oppression fulfilled)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad III</strong></td>\n<td>Elisha's dying prophecy concerned victory over him (2 Kings 13:14-19)</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:24</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Rezin</strong></td>\n<td>Isaiah prophesied his downfall (Isaiah 7:4-8; 8:4)</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>2 Kings 16:9</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"narrative-events-fulfilled-prophecies-bible-portions-2\"><strong>Narrative: Events, Fulfilled Prophecies &amp; Bible Portions</strong></h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>King</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Died After Which King (Other/Same Kingdom)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Bible Portions About the King (All)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Events in Their Time</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Fulfilled Prophecies</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Important Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Rezon</strong></td>\n<td>Contemporary of Solomon (Judah/Israel undivided)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 11:23-25</td>\n<td>Fled from his master Hadadezer of Zobah, gathered a band of raiders, and seized Damascus to found an independent Syrian kingdom.</td>\n<td>Part of the fulfillment of God's warning that He would raise up adversaries against Solomon for his idolatry (1 Kings 11:11, 14, 23).</td>\n<td>Founder of the independent Aramean kingdom at Damascus that would trouble Israel and Judah for the next two centuries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hezion</strong></td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:18 (mentioned only in a genealogy)</td>\n<td>Known only as the grandfather of Ben-hadad I in the genealogy \"Tabrimon, son of Hezion, king of Syria.\" Some scholars identify Hezion with Rezon as the same person under two names.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Almost nothing is recorded of his reign beyond his name in a lineage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Tabrimon</strong></td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:18 (mentioned only in a genealogy)</td>\n<td>Father of Ben-hadad I; no acts are recorded, only his place in the royal genealogy of Syria.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Like Hezion, known only by name.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad I</strong></td>\n<td>Contemporary of Asa (Judah) and Baasha/Omri (Israel)</td>\n<td>1 Kings 15:18-20; 2 Chron 16:1-6</td>\n<td>Bribed with gold and silver from the Temple and palace treasuries by King Asa of Judah to break his treaty with Baasha of Israel; attacked and conquered several northern Israelite cities (Ijon, Dan, Abel-beth-maachah, and all Naphtali), forcing Baasha to withdraw from fortifying Ramah.</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Note: many scholars debate whether this Ben-hadad is the same individual as the Ben-hadad who later fought Ahab (sometimes numbered Ben-hadad II), given the long span of years involved; numbering conventions vary between sources.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad (II)*</strong></td>\n<td>Contemporary of Ahab, Ahaziah, and Jehoram of Israel; Jehoshaphat of Judah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 20; 1 Kings 22:1-4; 2 Kings 6:24-7:20; 2 Kings 8:7-15</td>\n<td>Besieged Samaria twice under Ahab but was defeated both times, then spared and given a covenant by Ahab; later besieged Samaria again under Jehoram of Israel, causing catastrophic famine, but his army fled in panic per Elisha's prophecy; fell sick, sent his officer Hazael to ask Elisha if he would recover, and was murdered by Hazael on the way home.</td>\n<td>An unnamed prophet warned Ahab that sparing this king's life would cost Ahab his own life and his people's (1 Kings 20:42) - a warning that contributed to Ahab's eventual downfall.</td>\n<td>His death by Hazael's hand fulfilled Elijah's original commission to anoint Hazael as king over Syria (1 Kings 19:15).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Hazael</strong></td>\n<td>Contemporary of Jehu, Jehoahaz of Israel; Jehoash of Judah</td>\n<td>1 Kings 19:15-17; 2 Kings 8:7-15, 28-29; 9:14-15; 10:32-33; 12:17-18; 13:3, 22, 24-25</td>\n<td>Usurped the Syrian throne by murdering Ben-hadad; oppressed Israel severely for decades under Jehu and Jehoahaz, reducing Israel's army to almost nothing; captured Gath and marched on Jerusalem under Jehoash of Judah, who paid him off with Temple and palace treasures to withdraw.</td>\n<td>Elijah's commission to anoint Hazael as a future scourge on Israel (1 Kings 19:15, 17) was fulfilled; Elisha's tearful prophecy of the specific atrocities Hazael would commit against Israel (2 Kings 8:12) was fulfilled in his decades of oppression.</td>\n<td>Historically confirmed outside Scripture by the Tel Dan Stele (which names the \"House of David\") and by Assyrian records of Shalmaneser III, which describe him as a usurper with 'no royal blood.'</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ben-hadad III</strong></td>\n<td>Contemporary of Jehoash (Joash) of Israel</td>\n<td>2 Kings 13:3, 24-25</td>\n<td>Succeeded his father Hazael; defeated three times in battle by Jehoash of Israel, who recovered the Israelite cities his father Hazael had taken.</td>\n<td>Elisha's dying prophecy that Jehoash would strike Syria exactly three times (2 Kings 13:18-19) was fulfilled precisely (2 Kings 13:25).</td>\n<td>His defeats marked the beginning of Syria's decline as a regional power, paving the way for Jeroboam II's territorial expansion in Israel.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Rezin</strong></td>\n<td>Contemporary of Pekah of Israel and Ahaz of Judah</td>\n<td>2 Kings 15:37; 16:5-9; Isaiah 7:1-8; 8:4; 9:11</td>\n<td>Last king of independent Syria; allied with Pekah of Israel against Ahaz of Judah in the Syro-Ephraimite War, besieging Jerusalem; Ahaz appealed to Assyria's Tiglath-Pileser III for help instead of trusting God as Isaiah urged; Tiglath-Pileser marched on Damascus and killed Rezin, ending Syria as an independent nation.</td>\n<td>Isaiah's prophecy that Rezin's plan against Judah would not succeed and that Syria's power (with Ephraim/Israel) would be broken within 65 years (Isaiah 7:7-8) was fulfilled at Damascus's fall in 732 BC; Isaiah 8:4 that Damascus's riches would be carried off by Assyria before a child could say 'my father, my mother' was also fulfilled.</td>\n<td>His death and Damascus's fall permanently ended Syria (Aram-Damascus) as an independent kingdom - it became an Assyrian province.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"b\">Every northern kings is recorded as evil - none removed the 'sin of Jeroboam' (the golden calf worship).</p>\n<p class=\"b\">Judah had a mix of good and evil kings; the clearly good kings were Asa, Jehoshaphat, Jehoash (early), Amaziah (partially), Uzziah, Jotham, Hezekiah, and Josiah.</p>\n"}]}];